                <pb facs="1194" n="1036" sameAs="frus:frus1906p2/pg1036"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Wright to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1906-07-06"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">July 6, 1906</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 23.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose herewith
                    translations of a number of imperial ordinances from the Official Gazette,
                    relating to the organization, powers, and procedure of the Japanese courts in
                    Korea.</p>
                <p>It will be noted that the laws now promulgated provided for two classes of
                    courts—courts of first instance and courts of appeal. The resident-general is
                    invested with certain judicial power, such as are exercised by the respective
                    ministers of state in Japan. The organization of the courts is determined and
                    regulations relating to both criminal and civil procedure are set forth.</p>
                <p>An editorial in the Asahi Shimbun raises the question as to the ultimate effect
                    of the new judicial system upon the ex-territorial jurisdiction now exercised by
                    the treaty powers in Korea. I have the honor to inclose a translation of the
                    editorial for the department’s information.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Luke E. Wright.</hi></signed></closer>






